@* The body of a page, below its bars.

   Every page used to invent this. Seven different containers — p-3 with one of four inline
   max-widths, p-3 bare, p-4, or nothing at all — and the reading measure, which is the single most
   consequential typographic decision on a text-heavy page, was an inline style chosen ad hoc:
   fifteen distinct values across twenty-six declarations.

   Three named measures replace them. A page picks what its content IS, not how many rem that
   happens to be, so the scale can be retuned in one place.

   Written in logical properties. Localisation is deferred rather than dropped, and right-to-left
   is the half of it that CSS has to answer; doing it while this is being written costs nothing and
   is a second pass through every page if it is not. *@

<div class="page-body @MeasureClass @(Flush ? "flush" : null) @Class">@ChildContent</div>

@code {
    public enum Width
    {
        /// <summary>No cap. For a card grid, which should use whatever room it is given.</summary>
        Full,
        /// <summary>A form or a single column of controls.</summary>
        Form,
        /// <summary>Prose meant to be read start to finish.</summary>
        Text,
        /// <summary>Tables, charts and figures, which need room but not the whole screen.</summary>
        Wide,
    }

    [Parameter] public Width Measure { get; set; } = Width.Full;

    /// <summary>No padding either — the content reaches the screen edge, as a grid should.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Flush { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    private string? MeasureClass => Measure switch
    {
        Width.Form => "measure-form",
        Width.Text => "measure-text",
        Width.Wide => "measure-wide",
        _ => null,
    };
}
